>
  <p>
    Use this template when reacting to a paper or technical post. Embed at most
    one or two figures — readers want <em>your</em> reading, not a re-render.
    Quote sparingly.
  </p>

  <h2>The paper in one paragraph</h2>

  <p>
    Open with a one-paragraph summary that you would tell someone in a hallway.
    No setup, no preamble, no "in this work the authors propose…". Just the
    claim.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      We apply Okapi BM25 scoring to sparse visual word activations from a
      Sparse Autoencoder on Vision Transformer patch tokens, enabling efficient,
      interpretable image retrieval that matches dense accuracy via a two-stage
      pipeline.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What's new</h2>

  <p>
    Three things, ordered by how much I think they matter:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Sparse visual words actually behave Zipfian.</strong> If you've
      worked in IR, this is the key claim — it's why BM25's IDF is the right
      tool, not a marketing analogy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two-stage pipeline recovers near-exact accuracy.</strong> First-stage
      sparse retrieval at high recall, then dense rerank on the top-200.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Attribution is exact.</strong> No saliency hacks, no Grad-CAM. The
      score decomposes by visual word.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The figure that does the work</h2>

  <figure>
    <img src="/bm25v/img/rank_freq_overlay.png" alt="Rank-frequency plot of visual words across 7 datasets" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      Visual word rank-frequency across seven datasets. Near-parallel power-law
      tails are the entire empirical case for using BM25 over a learned scorer.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Pick one figure. The figure should be the one that, if removed, would
    weaken the paper's argument the most. Almost never the architecture diagram.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd want next</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Does the Zipfian fit hold on heterogeneous galleries (e.g., LAION-style scale)?</li>
    <li>Can the SAE be replaced with a TopK ReLU bottleneck and still preserve attribution?</li>
    <li>What does the latency curve look like at 100M+ images, where HNSW build time becomes the bottleneck?</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout-title">Hot take</p>
    <p>The paper's strongest contribution isn't speed — it's making attribution exact. Speed is a side-effect.</p>
  </div>

